\f1\b0\fs24 \cf0 There is a difference between understanding something and keeping it coherent while it changes. Most systems\'97conversations, theories, decisions, even identities\'97fail not because they are wrong, but because they lose coherence as they move. Definitions freeze meaning; beliefs defend it. Neither survives motion very well. What does survive is structure: a set of constraints that allow change without collapse.\
A canon is that structure. It does not explain reality or prescribe behavior. It simply establishes what must remain intact for anything to stay intelligible while it evolves. When thought, language, or action moves within such constraints, meaning doesn\'92t need to be enforced\'97it is preserved automatically. When those constraints are violated, things don\'92t become false; they short-circuit. Confusion, contradiction, and loss of purpose follow not as moral failures, but as structural ones.\
This is why instruments last longer than explanations. A musical staff does not tell you what music is, yet it allows infinite music without breaking coherence. A coordinate plane does not tell you where to go, yet any motion within it remains legible. A canon functions the same way for meaning: it is a geometry of allowable motion. When you align with it, action feels effortless and purpose clarifies. When you fight it, friction increases. Nothing mystical is required\'97only respect for the difference between defining something and allowing it to move lawfully.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl360\slmult1\partightenfactor0

